<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<chapter xml:id="fujita-overview">
  <title>Fujita’s freeness conjecture</title>
  <shorttitle>The conjecture</shorttitle>
  <introduction>
    <p>These notes present OpenAI's argument.<fn>These notes are a rewritten version of OpenAI's article <url href="https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Fujitas-freeness-conjecture-September-23-2026/Fujitas-freeness-conjecture-September-23-2026.pdf"><em>Fujita's freeness conjecture</em></url> and are intended solely for personal study.</fn></p>
    <p>Let <m>X</m> be a smooth complex projective variety of dimension <m>n</m>, and let <m>L</m> be an ample line bundle. We prove that <m>K_X+mL</m> is globally generated for every integer <m>m\geq n+1</m>. At a hypothetical base point <m>x</m> of <m>K_X+(n+1)L</m>, the proof minimizes an exponential functional of section orders and log discrepancies at a fixed section degree. A uniform estimate for the images of restriction maps excludes positive-dimensional centers of minimizing valuations. A minimizing pair whose valuation has center <m>\{x\}</m> determines a rational boundary to which Kawamata–Viehweg vanishing applies.</p>
    <p>We use additive notation for line bundles.<fn>Thus <m>jL</m> denotes the <m>j</m>th tensor power of <m>L</m>, and addition of line bundles denotes tensor product.</fn> Fujita's freeness conjecture predicts that <m>K_X+(n+1)L</m> is globally generated whenever <m>X</m> is a smooth projective variety of dimension <m>n</m> and <m>L</m> is ample <xref ref="bib-fujita1987"/>. No global generation or nonvanishing assumption is imposed on <m>L</m> itself.</p>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-main">
      <statement>
        <p>Let <m>X</m> be a smooth connected projective variety over <m>\C</m> of dimension <m>n\geq1</m>, and let <m>L</m> be an ample line bundle on <m>X</m>. Then <m>K_X+(n+1)L</m> is generated by its global sections.</p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>
    <p>Connectedness only fixes the notation: one applies the assertion separately to each connected component.<fn>In dimension zero every line bundle is globally generated.</fn> The bound is sharp, since <m>K_{\mathbb P^n}=-(n+1)\cO_{\mathbb P^n}(1)</m>. Thus the adjoint bundle is trivial at <m>m=n+1</m>, whereas at <m>m=n</m> it has no nonzero sections.</p>
    <p>For global generation it suffices to find, at each closed point <m>x</m>, a global section with nonzero value in the fiber at <m>x</m>.<fn>Separation of points and tangent directions, including the very-ampleness assertion in Fujita's conjecture, requires further conclusions.</fn> Theorem <xref ref="thm-main" text="global"/> also gives generation for every <m>m\geq n+1</m>. Corollary <xref ref="cor-all-powers" text="global"/> deduces this by applying the theorem to a product with projective space. The argument applies even when <m>L</m> is not globally generated.</p>
  </introduction>
  <section>
    <title>Previous results</title>
    <p>Riemann–Roch proves the assertion for curves. Reider proved the surface case <xref ref="bib-reider1988"/>, Ein–Lazarsfeld the threefold case <xref ref="bib-einlazarsfeld1993"/>, Kawamata the fourfold case <xref ref="bib-kawamata1997"/>, and Ye–Zhu the fivefold case <xref ref="bib-yezhu2020"/>. In arbitrary dimension, Angehrn–Siu obtained the bound <m>m\geq(n^2+n+2)/2</m> <xref ref="bib-angehrnsiu1995"/>. Helmke established multiplicity estimates for the centers occurring in the vanishing argument <xref ref="bib-helmke1997"/>, <xref ref="bib-helmke1999"/>. Heier used these estimates together with the Angehrn–Siu method to obtain a bound of order <m>n^{4/3}</m> <xref ref="bib-heier2002"/>. Ghidelli–Lacini proved generation for <m>m\geq n(\log\log n+2.34)</m> when <m>n\geq2</m> <xref ref="bib-ghidellilacini2024" detail="Theorem 1.1"/>. Han's preprint gives <m>m\geq\ceil{C_0n}</m>, where <m>C_0=1.77629988\ldots</m> <xref ref="bib-han2026" detail="Theorem 1.1"/>.</p>
    <p>Chan announced a proof of the stronger intersection-number formulation <xref ref="bib-chan2024" detail="Theorem 1.3"/>.<fn>The preprint was withdrawn when the local extension used in that argument was found not to preserve the required order of vanishing. The withdrawal is recorded in the second arXiv version.</fn></p>
    <p>The vanishing arguments of Ein–Lazarsfeld <xref ref="bib-einlazarsfeld1993"/> and Kawamata <xref ref="bib-kawamata1997"/> construct singularities at a prescribed point, reduce a center to that point, and lift a nonzero fiber value. A positive-dimensional center may be singular, and reducing its dimension uses positivity. Angehrn–Siu used a smooth moving point and semicontinuity <xref ref="bib-angehrnsiu1995"/>. Fujita estimated the ranks of restrictions to the exceptional divisor of the point blowup <xref ref="bib-fujita1993"/>. Helmke instead controlled the multiplicities of log canonical centers <xref ref="bib-helmke1997"/>, <xref ref="bib-helmke1999"/>.</p>
    <p>Here the center is constrained by a minimizing condition. The growth of restrictions to the center gives a lower bound for a tangential moment, while the first variation at a minimizer gives an incompatible upper bound. The restriction estimate is uniform over all integral subvarieties of a fixed dimension, including singular subvarieties of unbounded degree. The final section is obtained by the local discrepancy and vanishing argument of Fujita <xref ref="bib-fujita1993" detail="§1(1.6)"/>.</p>
    <p>The convex constructions are related to the initial-exponent semigroups studied by Okounkov <xref ref="bib-okounkov2003"/>, Lazarsfeld–Mustaţă <xref ref="bib-lazarsfeldmustata2009"/>, and Kaveh–Khovanskii <xref ref="bib-kavehkhovanskii2012"/>. Boucksom–Chen study distributions of filtration jumps <xref ref="bib-boucksomchen2011"/>. Blum–Jonsson prove attainment for valuative log canonical and stability thresholds of ample line bundles <xref ref="bib-blumjonsson2020" detail="Theorem E"/>. We establish attainment separately for the exponential functional used here.</p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <title>Notation and the argument</title>
    <shorttitle>Notation and argument</shorttitle>
    <p>Throughout, <m>X</m>, <m>L</m>, and <m>n</m> satisfy Theorem <xref ref="thm-main" text="global"/>. We denote the adjoint bundle by <m>P=K_X+(n+1)L</m>, and write <m>R_j=H^0(X,jL)</m> and <m>N_j=\dim R_j</m>. Since <m>X</m> is smooth and connected, it is integral. All centers of valuations are closed centers: a valuation centered along a subvariety <m>W</m> is admissible at every point of <m>W</m>.</p>
    <p>The local generation criterion in <xref ref="sec-generation-criterion" text="type-global"/> states the divisor conditions needed for the final lift. On a smooth model <m>\rho:T\to X</m>, it is enough to construct an effective rational divisor <m>B</m> with simple normal crossing (SNC) support such that <m>(n+1)\rho^*L-B</m> is nef and big. There must also be a nonempty reduced divisor <m>S</m> whose components map to <m>x</m> and have coefficients in <m>B</m> equal to their log discrepancies. Every other prime divisor meeting <m>S</m> must have coefficient in <m>B</m> strictly less than its log discrepancy.</p>
    <p>Suppose that <m>P</m> is not generated at <m>x</m>. The mean of the total degrees of the ordinary initial exponents, divided by the section degree, then has lower limit strictly greater than <m>n/(n+1)</m>, by Proposition <xref ref="prop-ordinary-gap" text="global"/>. This strictness is proved by analyzing the lattice equality case and lifting from the exceptional divisor of the blowup of <m>x</m>.</p>
    <p>For a positive integer <m>m</m> with <m>N_m&gt;0</m>, a basis <m>\mathbf s=(s_i)</m> of <m>R_m</m>, and a nonzero monomial valuation <m>v</m>, write <m>\A(v)</m> for its log discrepancy, scaled in the same way as <m>v</m>, and consider<md number="no">\Phi_{m,t}(v,\mathbf s)=\frac1{N_m}\sum_{i=1}^{N_m}\exp\!\left(\frac{\A(v)}t-\frac{v(s_i)}m\right).</md> The parameter <m>t</m> will be fixed below <m>n+1</m>. The infimum of <m>\Phi_{m,t}</m> over bases and valuations whose closed centers contain <m>x</m> is denoted by <m>\lambda_{m,t}(x)</m>. The strict ordinary-order inequality gives <m>\lambda_{m,t}(x)\leq1-\delta</m> for some fixed <m>\delta&gt;0</m>, uniformly in sufficiently large <m>m</m> and in <m>t</m> near <m>n+1</m>. Every admissible pair <m>(v,\mathbf s)</m> satisfying <m>\Phi_{m,t}\leq1-\delta/2</m> has <m>\A(v)</m> in a fixed interval <m>[a_-,a_+]\subset(0,\infty)</m>, uniformly in the same ranges. These bounds therefore apply to every minimizing pair and to all sufficiently late terms of every minimizing sequence.</p>
    <p>To obtain a minimum, <xref first="sec-min-exponential" last="fujita-attainment" text="type-global"/> expresses simultaneous inequalities on section orders as threshold conditions on a complete flag variety. Properness of the flag variety and closedness of these loci give a single flag and an adapted basis. Retraction to a log resolution of the divisors of this basis then reduces the remaining limit to a compact simplex of monomial weights.</p>
    <p>In <xref first="fujita-restrictions" last="sec-centers" text="type-global"/>, a tangential variation at a positive-dimensional center bounds a weighted first moment from above. Sections restricted to the center give pure tangential initial exponents. Their growth, estimated uniformly and combined with the Brunn–Minkowski inequality, gives a lower bound incompatible with the variation inequality. One fixed choice of <m>t&lt;n+1</m> therefore forces every minimizing center to be <m>\{x\}</m> in arbitrarily large degrees.</p>
    <p>A minimizing pair with <m>c_X(v)=\{x\}</m> determines a supporting functional on finitely many rational polytopes of normalized section orders. Its coefficients are proportional to the negative gradient of <m>\Phi_{m,t}</m> viewed as a function of these normalized orders with the discrepancy held fixed. The vanishing derivative under positive rescaling determines their normalization. <xref ref="sec-isolation" text="type-global"/> selects rational supporting data and perturbs them by a coherent ideal. The perturbation makes the boundary coefficient equal to the log discrepancy on each component of a reduced divisor <m>S</m> and gives the local conditions of <xref ref="sec-generation-criterion" text="type-global"/>. Kawamata–Viehweg vanishing then produces a section of <m>P</m> nonzero at <m>x</m>. This construction requires control of the boundary near <m>S</m> and allows the correction divisor used in descent to have negative components away from <m>S</m>.</p>
    <table xml:id="fig-proof-route">
      <title>The contradiction yielding the sharp adjoint bound. Discrepancy bounds are obtained before the choice of <m>t&lt;n+1</m> and remain uniform in the degree. For the chosen parameter, minimizers with center <m>\{x\}</m> occur in arbitrarily large degrees. The divisor <m>S</m> is the reduced equality divisor over <m>x</m>.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" valign="top">
        <row>
          <cell>Assumed base point</cell>
          <cell>Normalized ordinary mean has <m>\liminf&gt;n/(n+1)</m>, by Proposition <xref ref="prop-ordinary-gap" text="global"/>.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Attained minimum</cell>
          <cell><m>0&lt;\lambda_{m,t}(x)&lt;1</m> and <m>0&lt;a_-\leq\A(v)\leq a_+</m>, by Proposition <xref ref="prop-minimizers" text="global"/>.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Point center</cell>
          <cell>Every minimizer has center <m>\{x\}</m> for arbitrarily large degrees, by Proposition <xref ref="prop-point-center" text="global"/>.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Rational isolation</cell>
          <cell>Boundary coefficients equal log discrepancies on <m>S</m> and are strictly smaller on the other components meeting <m>S</m>, by Lemma <xref ref="lem-isolated-equality" text="global"/>.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Nonzero fiber value</cell>
          <cell>The criterion of <xref ref="sec-generation-criterion" text="type-global"/> lifts from <m>S</m> and descends to <m>s(x)\ne0</m>.</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </section>
</chapter>
